<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">COP28</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2023">2023</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as 2023 United Nations Climate Change Conference</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">COP28, the 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> Climate Change Conference, was held at Expo City Dubai in the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> from 30 November to 13 December 2023 under the presidency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Al Jaber, head of the national oil company. Its final agreement, the UAE Consensus, called for the first time for 'transitioning away from fossil fuels in energy systems', and the conference launched a loss and damage fund for vulnerable countries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cop28.jpg" alt="COP28" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Palácio do Planalto from Brasilia, Brasil · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:15_11_2021_Expo_Dubai_2020_(51683229098).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The United Nations Climate Change Conference is held each year for the countries that signed the 1994 United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change, with the host rotating among members. In early 2021 the United Arab Emirates offered to host the 2023 event, and Dubai was chosen as the host city in November of that year. It was the third conference hosted by a member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opec">OPEC</a>. The UAE committed to net zero emissions by 2050, the first Middle Eastern government to make such a pledge.</p><p>Before the summit the climate envoys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> met regularly, and on 15 November the United States and China announced an agreement to triple global renewable energy use by 2030.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>COP28 was the largest climate summit to that date, with over 80,000 accredited participants, up from 49,000 the year before, among them more than 150 heads of state and government. King Charles III gave the opening speech, saying the world was dreadfully far off track in meeting its climate targets. Numerous sessions treated the effect of climate change on public health, a first for a conference of the parties, and it was the first to discuss food systems.</p><p>The UAE Declaration on Sustainable Agriculture, Resilient Food Systems, and Climate Action was adopted by 159 countries. The conference was criticised for the choice of Sultan Al Jaber, chief executive of the Abu Dhabi National Oil Company, as its president, and for the size of the meeting's carbon footprint.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A first draft of the final agreement, released on 11 December, was widely rejected by most Western countries because it avoided calling for a fossil fuel phase-out. The Alliance of Small Island States called it a death certificate for small island nations, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opec">OPEC</a> urged participants to reject any mention of phasing out fossil fuels. The conference, scheduled to end on 12 December, was extended after Saudi objections.</p><p>On 13 December Al Jaber announced a compromise, the UAE Consensus, the first in the history of the summits to mention the need to move away from every type of fossil fuel. It called for a tripling of global renewable energy capacity by 2030, further efforts towards the phase-down of unabated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> power and a cut in methane emissions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> did not sign the pledge on renewable energy. The head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">International Monetary Fund</a> welcomed the creation of the loss and damage fund.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “2023 United Nations Climate Change Conference” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
